  "verse": 1,
  "plain_english": "`pilot_V1A_ARGUMENT_MAP.md`. Dyczkowski-mode.*\n\n**The spontaneity of the Lord: the self-aware light**\n\nThis is where the whole Īśvarapratyabhijñāvivṛtivimarśinī begins — with the spontaneity, the\nsvācchandya, of the Lord. And the doctrine is announced in its fullness from the first lines. The blue\nand the yellow and the jar are not things that a light shines upon from outside; they shine forth\n*as* the light, without abandoning their own being, and are one with the manifestation itself. This —\nthat the world shines as the consciousness, and that the consciousness shines as the world — is the\nLord's spontaneity.\n\nThe Lord is of the nature of consciousness. He shines forth as the whole variety of knowers and knowns,\nwith a body that appears as if it were more than consciousness even though it is not more; and — this is\ndecisive — he is *aware* of shining. The image Abhinavagupta uses is the mirror. A mirror reflects the\nwall, the house, the elephant, the horse, the person, the pot, the potter — all the distinctions of\npart and whole, support and supported, cause and effect — without being more than its own smooth\nsurface. But the mirror does not know that it reflects. That is its only limit; it is inert. The Lord\nis the mirror that *knows*: he shines forth as all things, and he is aware that he does so, because\nconsciousness culminates in reflexive-awareness. The light that shines is the light that knows itself\nshining.\n\nAnd this answers the obvious objection: \"then it would always shine.\" For the \"always\" has no\nindependent time against which it could fail. What we call time is just the sun's movement — the\nshining of things in their succession. To ask \"why does the world shine as the sun moves?\" is like\nasking \"why does the blue not shine as yellow?\" It is the nature of the light to shine; there is no\ntime outside it that could make it shine or not. So the Lord is: *śaśvat*, un-cut by time; *acchanna*,\nnever becoming other than his own form; *svaccha*, clear — able to appear as more than himself while\nbeing only himself; and *svacchanda*, spontaneous — free of all dependence on another, resting in his\nown pulsation.\n\nAnd so the opening of the work is a prostration. The author bows to Śiva — the param-eśvara who removes\nthe delusion of the world, who is the essence of all good, the bliss and the liberation. And the\nprostration, Abhinavagupta explains, is not an empty word. It is the samāveśa, the entry: the\nabandoning of the bound knower's conceit, the subordination of the \"I\" of the world, and the rising of\nthe self-aware consciousness. Even the word \"namaḥ,\" the bowing, does its work not by being offered as\na bare sound but by pleasing the Lord — as the oblation's svāhā kindles the flame and gives to the\ndeity.\n\nSo the whole teaching is here, in the first opening: the Lord is the self-aware light, the mirror that\nknows, the spontaneity that shines as the world and is aware of shining; and the recognition of this —\nthe entry into this self-aware Śiva — is the whole of the liberation the work will unfold.\n\n- **Preserved:** the blue-and-the-rest shining as the manifestation (prakāśābhedamaya); the\n  svācchandya as the consciousness shining as the varied knowers/knowns and being aware of shining;\n  the mirror-analogy (wall, house, elephant, horse, person, pot, potter; the distinctions of part/\n  whole, support/supported, cause/effect); the mirror's un-awareness as its inertia; the param-eśvara\n  aware of himself (saṃvido vimarśaparyantatvāt); the time-objection and the answer (time = the sun's\n  movement; no independent time; the blue-as-yellow; the day of dissolution); the four epithets\n  (śaśvat, acchanna, svaccha, svacchanda) and their glosses; the prostration as the samāveśa (the\n  abandoning of the māyā-knower's conceit); the \"namaḥ\" as an indicator, by the Gītā-rule; the svāhā\n  and the oblation.\n- **Added (SUPPLIED, recoverable):** \"the mirror that knows\" (the svaccha + vimarśa synthesis);\n  \"the light that shines is the light that knows itself shining\" (restates the thesis); \"It is the\n  nature of the light to shine\" (glosses the time-answer). Interpretive unpacking, not new doctrine.\n- **NOT resolved:** the precise force of the four epithets' technical glosses; the \"a-śiva-rūpa-\n  sāṃsārika-moha\" soteriology. Logged, not invented.",
  "sanskrit_cite": "iha | punarnīlādayo'nujjhitanīlādibhāvā | eva | prakāśamānāḥ | santaḥ | prakāśābhedamayāḥ | | | tadidameva | bhagavataḥ | svācchandyam-yat | saṃvidātmaka | eva",
